# AppState Management Patterns ✅

## Core State Architecture

### Centralized State Management - [AppState](mdc:src-tauri/src/state.rs)
The Juno app uses a **centralized state management** pattern with Tauri's state system. All shared application state is managed through the `AppState` struct.

```rust
#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct AppState {
    pub desktop: Option<Arc<Desktop>>,
    pub memory_manager: Arc<TokioMutex<Box<dyn MemoryManager + Send + Sync>>>,
    pub timer_manager: Arc<TokioMutex<TimerManager>>,
    pub last_window_operation: Arc<TokioMutex<Option<Instant>>>,
    pub voice_transcription: Arc<TokioMutex<VoiceTranscriptionState>>,
}
```

## State Access Patterns

### 1. Safe Desktop Access
The desktop instance is optional to handle permission failures gracefully:

```rust
impl AppState {
    /// Get desktop instance with error handling
    pub fn get_desktop(&self) -> Result<Arc<Desktop>, String> {
        self.desktop.as_ref()
            .ok_or_else(|| "Desktop automation not available. Please grant accessibility permissions.".to_string())
            .map(|d| Arc::clone(d))
    }

    /// Check if desktop automation is available
    pub fn is_desktop_available(&self) -> bool {
        self.desktop.is_some()
    }

    /// Try to get desktop instance (returns Option)
    pub fn try_get_desktop(&self) -> Option<Arc<Desktop>> {
        self.desktop.as_ref().map(|d| Arc::clone(d))
    }
}
```

### 2. Memory Manager Access
```rust
impl AppState {
    /// Get memory manager for agent operations
    pub async fn get_memory_manager(&self) -> Arc<TokioMutex<Box<dyn MemoryManager + Send + Sync>>> {
        Arc::clone(&self.memory_manager)
    }

    /// Add message to conversation history
    pub async fn add_message(&self, role: String, content: String) -> Result<(), String> {
        let memory = self.memory_manager.lock().await;
        memory.add_message(role, content).await
            .map_err(|e| format!("Failed to add message to memory: {}", e))
    }
}
```

### 3. Timer Management
```rust
impl AppState {
    /// Get timer manager for long-running operations
    pub fn get_timer_manager(&self) -> Arc<TokioMutex<TimerManager>> {
        Arc::clone(&self.timer_manager)
    }

    /// Start a new timer
    pub async fn start_timer(&self, duration: Duration, context: String) -> Result<String, String> {
        let mut timer_manager = self.timer_manager.lock().await;
        timer_manager.start_timer(duration, context).await
    }
}
```

## DesktopWrapper Pattern

### Graceful Command Forwarding
When desktop automation is unavailable, commands still work but return helpful errors:

```rust
pub struct DesktopWrapper {
    desktop: Option<Arc<Desktop>>,
}

impl DesktopWrapper {
    pub fn new(desktop: Option<Arc<Desktop>>) -> Self {
        Self { desktop }
    }

    pub async fn click(&self, x: f64, y: f64) -> Result<(), String> {
        match &self.desktop {
            Some(desktop) => {
                desktop.click(x, y).await
                    .map_err(|e| format!("Click failed: {}", e))
            }
            None => {
                Err("Desktop automation not available. Please grant accessibility permissions in System Preferences > Security & Privacy > Accessibility.".to_string())
            }
        }
    }

    // Similar pattern for all other desktop methods...
}
```

## Command Integration Patterns

### 1. State Access in Commands
All Tauri commands receive AppState and access it safely:

```rust
#[tauri::command]
pub async fn desktop_command(
    state: State<'_, AppState>
) -> Result<ResponseType, String> {
    // Safe desktop access
    let desktop = state.get_desktop()
        .map_err(|e| format!("Desktop automation not available: {}", e))?;

    // Perform operation
    desktop.operation().await
        .map_err(|e| format!("Operation failed: {}", e))
}
```

### 2. Memory Operations in Commands
```rust
#[tauri::command]
pub async fn agent_command(
    message: String,
    state: State<'_, AppState>
) -> Result<String, String> {
    // Add user message to memory
    state.add_message("user".to_string(), message.clone()).await?;

    // Get memory manager for agent
    let memory_manager = state.get_memory_manager().await;

    // Perform agent operation...
    Ok(response)
}
```

### 3. Timer Operations in Commands
```rust
#[tauri::command]
pub async fn start_long_operation(
    context: String,
    state: State<'_, AppState>
) -> Result<String, String> {
    // Start timer for long operation
    let timer_id = state.start_timer(
        Duration::from_secs(300),
        context
    ).await?;

    // Perform operation...
    Ok(timer_id)
}
```

## State Initialization

### Application Startup - [lib.rs](mdc:src-tauri/src/lib.rs)
```rust
pub fn run() {
    // Initialize desktop with graceful fallback
    let desktop = match Desktop::new(false, true) {
        Ok(desktop) => {
            info!("Desktop automation engine initialized successfully");
            Some(Arc::new(desktop))
        }
        Err(e) => {
            warn!("Desktop automation engine failed to initialize: {}. App will continue with limited functionality.", e);
            None
        }
    };

    // Initialize memory manager
    let memory_manager: Arc<TokioMutex<Box<dyn MemoryManager + Send + Sync>>> =
        Arc::new(TokioMutex::new(Box::new(SimpleMemoryManager::new())));

    // Initialize timer manager
    let timer_manager = Arc::new(TokioMutex::new(TimerManager::new()));

    // Create AppState
    let app_state = AppState {
        desktop,
        memory_manager,
        timer_manager,
        last_window_operation: Arc::new(TokioMutex::new(None)),
        voice_transcription: Arc::new(TokioMutex::new(VoiceTranscriptionState::new())),
    };

    // Register with Tauri
    tauri::Builder::default()
        .manage(app_state)
        .run(tauri::generate_context!())
        .expect("error while running tauri application");
}
```

## Async State Management

### Mutex Usage Patterns
```rust
// Correct: Short-lived locks
{
    let mut timer_manager = state.timer_manager.lock().await;
    timer_manager.update_timer(id, status).await?;
} // Lock released here

// Correct: Clone Arc before async operations
let memory_manager = Arc::clone(&state.memory_manager);
tokio::spawn(async move {
    let mut memory = memory_manager.lock().await;
    memory.cleanup_old_messages().await;
});
```

### Avoiding Deadlocks
```rust
// Good: Single lock acquisition
async fn update_state(state: &AppState) -> Result<(), String> {
    let mut memory = state.memory_manager.lock().await;
    memory.add_message("system", "status update").await?;
    // Lock automatically released at end of scope
    Ok(())
}

// Bad: Multiple nested locks
async fn bad_example(state: &AppState) -> Result<(), String> {
    let memory = state.memory_manager.lock().await;
    let timer = state.timer_manager.lock().await; // Potential deadlock
    // ... operations
    Ok(())
}
```

## State Testing Patterns

### Unit Test Setup
```rust
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    fn create_test_state() -> AppState {
        AppState {
            desktop: None, // Test without desktop
            memory_manager: Arc::new(TokioMutex::new(Box::new(SimpleMemoryManager::new()))),
            timer_manager: Arc::new(TokioMutex::new(TimerManager::new())),
            last_window_operation: Arc::new(TokioMutex::new(None)),
            voice_transcription: Arc::new(TokioMutex::new(VoiceTranscriptionState::new())),
        }
    }

    #[tokio::test]
    async fn test_memory_operations() {
        let state = create_test_state();

        // Test adding message
        let result = state.add_message("user".to_string(), "test".to_string()).await;
        assert!(result.is_ok());

        // Verify message was added
        let memory = state.memory_manager.lock().await;
        let messages = memory.get_messages().await.unwrap();
        assert_eq!(messages.len(), 1);
    }
}
```

### Integration Test Patterns
```rust
#[tauri::test]
async fn test_command_with_state() {
    let app = tauri::test::mock_app();
    let state = app.state::<AppState>();

    // Test command behavior
    let result = desktop_command(state).await;

    // Verify expected behavior based on state
    if state.is_desktop_available() {
        assert!(result.is_ok());
    } else {
        assert!(result.is_err());
        assert!(result.unwrap_err().contains("accessibility permissions"));
    }
}
```

## Best Practices

### 1. State Access
- Always use provided helper methods (`get_desktop()`, `try_get_desktop()`)
- Check availability before operations when appropriate
- Handle `None` cases gracefully with helpful error messages

### 2. Memory Management
- Use `Arc::clone()` for sharing state across threads
- Keep mutex locks short-lived
- Don't hold locks across await points unless necessary

### 3. Error Handling
- Provide context in error messages
- Guide users on how to fix issues (permission prompts, etc.)
- Log state changes for debugging

### 4. Thread Safety
- All state components are thread-safe
- Use appropriate synchronization primitives
- Avoid blocking operations inside locks

## Monitoring and Debugging

### State Inspection
```rust
impl AppState {
    /// Get current state summary for debugging
    pub async fn debug_summary(&self) -> String {
        format!(
            "Desktop: {}, Memory: {} messages, Timers: {} active",
            if self.desktop.is_some() { "Available" } else { "Unavailable" },
            self.memory_manager.lock().await.message_count().await.unwrap_or(0),
            self.timer_manager.lock().await.active_timer_count()
        )
    }
}
```

### Logging State Changes
```rust
// Log state changes for debugging
info!("AppState initialized with desktop: {}", state.is_desktop_available());
debug!("State summary: {}", state.debug_summary().await);
```

This state management pattern ensures consistent, thread-safe access to application state while maintaining graceful degradation capabilities.
